                <pb facs="0879" n="786" sameAs="frus:frus1935v04/pg786"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">812.404/1510: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Mexico</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Daniels</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Mexico</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1935-02-04T17:00:00-06:00">February 4, 1935—5
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received 7:40 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>20. Referring to Department’s telegram No. 24, the Embassy has no information
                    tending to substantiate the allegations mentioned. On the contrary, to the best
                    of the Embassy’s knowledge and belief, the Mexican Government has shown
                    consistently every inclination within its laws to afford American citizens and
                    institutions fair treatment in the religious sense. Embassy has reported
                    consistently present religious conflict in detail and instructed the Consuls to
                    do likewise.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Daniels</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
